### 5. Data Refresh / Load Time vs MSTR — 7%
**Why:** Some MSTR Intelligent Cubes on large tables take a long time and a lot of data to load. Compare refresh effort and user response on the same source.

**Key difference**
| | MicroStrategy | Sigma |
|---|---|---|
| Data handling | Intelligent Cube imports query results into MSTR in-memory store; dashboards read from the cube | No import. Every interaction runs SQL in Snowflake/Databricks |
| "Refresh" | Cube publish / republish (full or incremental) | Live query each time; or materialization refresh (needs write access, F2) |
| Freshness | As fresh as the last cube publish | Current data on every query (unless cached/materialized) |
| Cross-user reuse | Cube shared by all users | Browser cache is per user; warehouse cache depends on auth mode (F8) |

**How to test**
1. Pick 2–3 heavy MSTR cubes (largest rows/size, longest publish). Record row count, size, source tables.
2. MSTR: record full publish time, incremental refresh time, and dashboard load time on the loaded cube.
3. Sigma (same tables, same warehouse size):
   - Live dashboard load (cold/warm) and filter/drill response.
   - Materialization refresh time — **only if write access is allowed**; otherwise mark "Blocked by constraint."
4. Run each test 3 times at similar warehouse load; average.
5. Record warehouse compute for each run.

**Results table**
| Dataset | Rows / Size | MSTR full publish | MSTR incremental | MSTR dashboard load | Sigma live (cold/warm) | Sigma filter/drill | Sigma materialization refresh | Compute per run |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

**Pass**
- Sigma user response on the heavy dataset is at or better than the MSTR cube-backed dashboard, OR the trade-off (no refresh wait vs. slower live queries) is accepted by users.

---

### 6. Heavy Transformation Query vs MSTR (Gate) — 10%
**Why:** Some MSTR reports/cubes depend on large, complex transforms (multi-pass SQL, Freeform SQL, level metrics). Prove Sigma can reproduce the logic, match results, and perform acceptably.

**Key difference**
| | MicroStrategy | Sigma |
|---|---|---|
| Logic engine | MSTR SQL engine generates multi-pass SQL; Freeform SQL supported | No engine of its own. Logic in Custom SQL, data models, or workbook formulas, pushed down to the warehouse |
| When transform runs | Once at cube publish (cube-backed) | Every load, filter, or drill — unless materialized (blocked if no write access) |
| Timeout | MSTR governing settings | Sigma connection timeout (live); warehouse timeout (materialization, F6) |

**How to test**
1. Pick 1–2 of the heaviest MSTR transform reports/cubes. Capture MSTR-generated SQL, pass count, rows returned, runtime.
2. Rebuild the logic in Sigma (Custom SQL and/or data model). List anything that can't be expressed (level metrics, multi-pass behavior, cross-source joins).
3. Compare logic parity, result accuracy (row counts + key totals), runtime (cold/warm/per-filter), generated SQL, and compute per run.
4. Multiply Sigma per-query compute by expected daily interactions, since the transform reruns each time.
5. Repeat 3 runs each; average.

**Results table**
| Report / Cube | Logic reproducible? | Results match? | MSTR runtime | Sigma cold | Sigma warm | Sigma per-filter | Compute per run | Est. daily compute |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

**Pass**
- 100% result match. Response time acceptable without materialization. Daily compute cost acceptable vs. the MSTR cube approach.

